# Line endings, decided once.
#
# This repository is developed on Windows and built on Linux and macOS CI. The
# suite reads source files as text and asserts on their exact contents, and
# several tests search for a snippet that spans two lines (`"...();\n  ..."`).
# On Windows the runner checked the tree out with CRLF, every one of those
# searches missed, and 17 tests failed in CI while passing on Linux with the
# same commit. The first Windows build died at the Test step because of it, and
# the log was not readable from outside, so the failure looked like code.
#
# The fix is here rather than in 17 tests: one rule that makes the working tree
# identical on every platform, so a test that spans a line is testing the code
# and not the checkout.
#
# `text=auto` normalises to LF in the repository. `eol=lf` forces LF in the
# working tree too, including on Windows. Nothing here needs CRLF: no fixture is
# compared byte-for-byte against a Windows-native file, and Node, Git, npm and
# the bundler all read LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary files must never be normalised: a rewritten byte is a corrupt binary.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.webp binary
*.wav binary
*.mp3 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip binary
*.tar binary
*.bz2 binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.node binary
*.asar binary

# A Windows batch file is the one thing that genuinely wants CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
